  "summary": "Foundation-model perturbation predictors are being audited for whether reported gains survive delta-based and design-aware evaluation.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread across these preprints is whether single-cell perturbation response predictions can be trusted, and how evaluation and representation choices determine the answer. The STATE audit reports that a low-rank attention bottleneck bounds reliability in the decoder. The Delta Gap preprint shows that global Pearson correlation inflates performance: GEARS reaches delta Pearson 0.076 against 0.267 for a mean-delta baseline on Replogle K562. The plate-design analysis reports that plate layout and well effects confound donor variation and benchmark evaluation, and proposes design-aware estimators. On the modeling side, BioMeme replaces a learned state-to-response layer with a memory of measured experiments, and CellCanon frames prediction, explanation and observation-driven updating as an agentic world model. What is new is that reliability audits now sit alongside architectures that depend on measured data or explicit context rather than on learned decoders alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
